JieLi AC63 SDK
--------------
The application is built against the JieLi AC63 SDK (https://github.com/Jieli-Tech/fw-AC63_BT_SDK,
commit fdc018de81629e21e594d677cf05f87d288be0f0), Apache License 2.0
(http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0; included as licenses/jieli-ac63-sdk.txt). The SDK is fetched by
tools/env/fetch_deps.sh and is not part of the repository.

Files in this repository derived from SDK files (Apache License 2.0). Each one names its source
file and lists our changes in its header:

  firmware/app/include/app_config.h                     from apps/hid/include/app_config.h
  firmware/app/board/board_smc_mixer_cfg.h              from apps/hid/board/br25/board_ac636n_demo_cfg.h
  firmware/app/board/board_smc_mixer_global_build_cfg.h from apps/hid/board/br25/board_ac636n_demo_global_build_cfg.h
  firmware/app/board/board_smc_mixer.c                  from apps/hid/board/br25/board_ac636n_demo.c
  firmware/app/config/lib_update_config.c               from apps/hid/config/lib_update_config.c
  firmware/app/Makefile                                 from apps/hid/board/br25/Makefile

The application image also contains SDK code: SDK source files compiled as they are (listed in
firmware/app/Makefile) and members of six of the SDK's prebuilt libraries in cpu/br25/liba
(btctrler.a, btstack.a, cpu.a, crypto_toolbox_Osize.a, system.a, update.a; the link maps of
2026-11-14, the controller with and without BLE-MIDI and the hardware test alike). The SDK's
repository states one license, its LICENSE file at the top, and no other terms for these
libraries (its README says nothing about licensing); we read that LICENSE as covering them.
A release package carries the SDK's LICENSE as licenses/jieli-ac63-sdk.txt.

The bootloader contains no SDK code.

GCC runtime (RP2040 key)
------------------------
The RP2040 recovery key firmware links libgcc from Debian's gcc-arm-none-eabi 12.2 (GPL v3 with
the GCC Runtime Library Exception, which lets the linked firmware be distributed under our
license without further conditions). It contains no C library and no Raspberry Pi SDK code.

JieLi toolchain
---------------
Device images link a few functions from the JieLi toolchain (clang 4.0.1 for pi32v2,
jieli-linux-toolchains-20260730.1, docs/build.md); the toolchain package carries no license or
notice files of its own. From the link maps of 2026-11-14:

  libc.a, libm.a   newlib 2.2.0 (_NEWLIB_VERSION in the toolchain's newlib.h; the archives name
                   src/newlib/newlib/... as their sources): memcmp, memcpy, memset, strchr,
                   strcmp, strcpy, strlen, strncmp, the ctype table (ctype_), ceil (s_ceil, from
                   fdlibm); the bootloader only memcpy and memset
  libcompiler-rt.a LLVM compiler-rt: adddf3, subdf3, muldf3, divdf3, comparedf2, fixdfsi,
                   fixunsdfsi, floatsidf, floatunsidf (double arithmetic; divdf3, fixdfsi and
                   floatsidf in the BLE-MIDI build only)

Their notices, copied unchanged from those versions' sources, are in licenses/newlib.txt and
licenses/compiler-rt.txt, which go into every release package with this file:

- newlib: ctype_.c is under the University of California's BSD license (Copyright (c) 1989 The
  Regents of the University of California), s_ceil.c under Sun's fdlibm notice (Copyright (C)
  1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc.), and the string functions, which carry no notice of their own,
  under COPYING.NEWLIB's notice (1) (Copyright (c) 1994-2009 Red Hat, Inc., the BSD License).
  ctype_.c's text still has the advertising clause; the University withdrew it for its software
  in 1999. memcpy, memset, strcmp and strcpy have no source named in the archive (no debug
  information): they may be the toolchain's own assembly rather than newlib's C.
- compiler-rt 4.0.1: the University of Illinois/NCSA Open Source License and the MIT license
  (Copyright (c) 2009-2016, and 2009-2015 in the MIT text, by the contributors listed in its
  CREDITS.TXT), at the user's choice;
  both ask that the notice go with a binary distribution.
